Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Collections wants a prioritisation model, but validation must separate defaul=
t risk from the effect of different contact strategies. Failure anecdotes are=
 beginning to drive decisions, although retries and late posting may be makin=
g the problem look larger than the affected-customer population. There are en=
ough data exceptions to make a polished average dangerous; preserve the unres=
olved cases.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if high-risk customers need tailored arrangeme=
nts rather than more attempts?
2. What evidence would instead support that some customers self-cure without =
intervention?
3. How do we rule out that contact channel changes promise-kept rates?

We looked at a version of this before. For the December 2021 review, please s=
how what changed and whether the earlier conclusion still holds. Please work =
towards whether a model can support treatment allocation and what experiment =
is needed next. A useful output would be a working Excel file with the except=
ion population and clear columns.

Use collections cases, promises to pay and recovery payments; customer contac=
ts, complaints and suggestions; transaction-level timestamps, channels, amoun=
ts, statuses and error context; the latest closed loan files available by the=
 request date, including affordability, pricing and origination risk fields. =
One caution: Observational data cannot prove treatment causality; recommend a=
 controlled test where appropriate.

